Acute-on-chronic minor blister formation from ill-fitting footwear to heel
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Fluid-filled heel vesicle, Erythematous border with chronic callus, Friction-induced localized tenderness

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-001
Reason for Visit: Acute heel pain impairing ambulation requiring urgent evaluation for possible soft tissue infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute focal pain inhibiting safe weight-bearing. Urgent assessment was required to exclude deep soft tissue infection and prevent structural epidermal compromise before the next available in-hours consultation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heel has a painful bubble and I cannot wear shoes or walk properly.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced following new tight-fitting footwear usage. Patient reports a focal visible fluid-filled epidermal vesicle on the posterior heel. A localized erythematous halo surrounds the vesicular border. Tenderness exacerbates upon direct mechanical pressure or friction. The epidermal roof is thin but currently intact. There is underlying hyperkeratotic skin indicating chronic friction. Pain resolves immediately upon removal of the offending footwear.
Functional Status: Independent but mobilizing with an antalgic gait.
Social/Safety Concerns: Limited ambulation capacity temporarily impacts community access.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 124/78 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, displaying localized pain behaviors upon heel strike.
Physical Exam: Dermatological assessment reveals a fluid-filled heel vesicle with an erythematous border and chronic callus. Clear serous transudate is visible within the vesicle. Palpation confirms friction-induced localized tenderness. Complete absence of surrounding cellulitis, induration, or purulent exudate. Distal neurovascular status is intact.
Environmental Observations: Narrow, rigid footwear noted in the residence. No significant trip hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: No current prescription medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute-on-chronic minor blister formation from ill-fitting footwear to heel - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Cellulitis - Ruled out due to: Absence of spreading erythema, systemic fever, and purulent exudate.

Achilles tendinopathy - Ruled out due to: Pain strictly localized to superficial epidermal layers rather than the tendinous structure.

Retrocalcaneal bursitis - Ruled out due to: Lack of deep, boggy swelling anterior to the Achilles tendon.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Patient presented with acute, mobility-limiting heel pain. Telephone triage raised a high index of suspicion for early cellulitis or complicated pressure injury. On-site clinical evaluation confirmed an intact friction blister without ascending infection. Urgent after-hours assessment was definitively required to exclude infective complications, initiate barrier protection, and implement pressure offloading to prevent imminent vesicle rupture and secondary infection that would likely occur if delayed until the next in-hours period.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Application of a protective hydrocolloid dressing. Instructed strict avoidance of mechanical deroofing.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply padded dressings to reduce local friction, resulting in immediate pain reduction. Adopt open-backed footwear to eliminate posterior heel contact, allowing the epidermis to heal without repeated trauma.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Hydrocolloid blister plasters, open-backed footwear.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days if the lesion fails to resolve or pain escalates.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department if spreading redness, purulent discharge, escalating severe pain, or systemic fever develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending clinical progression; conditions are dynamic and subject to change.
Clinical Course: The vesicle fluid is expected to reabsorb over 7 to 10 days, followed by epidermal shedding. Continued friction will delay resolution and provoke rupture.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you notice red streaks spreading up your leg, yellow pus, or if you develop a fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Hydrocolloid dressings protect the fragile epidermal roof, maintain a sterile environment, and prevent bacterial ingress.
Actionable Steps: Keep the lesion covered and dry. Change the dressing only when the edges lift. Avoid enclosed shoes until completely healed.

